  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nUpper level ridging builds across the Ohio Valley through mid week. \nMeanwhile shortwave energy ejects out of the Rockies into the Upper \nMidwest Wednesday into Thursday. This system will stay well \nnorthwest of our area though and ridging will remain in control of \nour weather through at least late week. Another trough ejecting out \nof the Rockies into the Central CONUS Friday into Saturday may sweep \na bit further east which could allow a cold front to sink southeast \ninto our area on Saturday. The upper system likely stays well north \nof us though and moisture looks limited along the frontal boundary. \nSo whatever precipitation we manage to receive likely would be \nrather light. The LREF grand ensemble only has a 15-20% chance at \nreceiving at least 0.25\" from this system and the NBM probabilities \nare even lower.\n\nSurface high pressure centered across the Ohio Valley will shift \neastward over the coming days which will lead to southerly flow \nreturning. This helps boost temperatures to above normal levels \nheading into mid week and even further by late week. There remains a \nhigh probability (70-80% chance) of highs reaching 70 Friday and \nSaturday. Latest guidance has also backed off on the cool down as we \nhead into the week of Thanksgiving. While temperatures should trend \ndown, we may stay close to normal for several days as the synoptic \npattern doesn't appear favorable for a true shot of colder air \nheading into the early part of next week.",
